  "activity": {
    "id": "act_dna_replication",
    "name": "The DNA Double Helix & Semiconservative Replication",
    "description": "A ten-step interactive exploration of DNA's three-dimensional structure and its semiconservative replication. Students begin with a schematic ladder, transition to the realistic double helix, examine antiparallel 5'-to-3' directionality, complete an interactive complementary base-pairing exercise, build up the replication fork enzyme by enzyme (helicase, SSBs, DNA polymerase on leading and lagging strands, and DNA ligase), and finish by predicting the outcome of successive rounds of semiconservative replication.",
    "workplace": "wp_dna_replication",
    "start": "step_ladder",
    "actions": [
      {
        "id": "step_ladder",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "1. The DNA Ladder \u2014 Backbones & Bases",
          "description": "Observe the flat, ladder-like schematic of DNA. The two vertical lines are the sugar-phosphate backbones; each horizontal rung is a base pair made of two colored, labeled segments (A red, T blue, G yellow, C green). Notice how the nitrogenous bases project inward to pair with their complements. Click Next to twist this ladder into a realistic helix."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_dna_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "aug_ladder_schematic",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_dna_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "label_backbone",
              "poi": "strand_left_end",
              "text": "Sugar-phosphate backbone"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_dna_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "label_base_pairs",
              "poi": "default",
              "text": "Base pairs: A-T, G-C"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_helix_stage",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narration_structure",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_dna_model",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_helix_stage",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_helix"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_helix",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "2. From Ladder to Double Helix",
          "description": "The ladder twists into the familiar double helix: the backbones spiral around a common axis while the base pairs stack along the interior. Real DNA completes about one full turn every ten base pairs. Rotate the model to observe the grooves that emerge from the geometry. Click Next to explore strand directionality."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_dna_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "aug_double_helix",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_helix_stage",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "label_backbone",
              "poi": "top_label_slot",
              "text": "One turn \u2248 10 base pairs"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_helix_stage",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narration_helix",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_dna_model",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_helix_stage",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_antiparallel"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_antiparallel",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "3. Antiparallel Strands & 5'-to-3' Direction",
          "description": "Arrows now run along each backbone: one strand points up, the other down. The 5' and 3' labels mark the sugar-carbon termini at the ends of each strand. The two strands ALWAYS run in opposite directions \u2014 this antiparallel arrangement is forced by the directional chemistry of the backbone. Rotate to confirm the arrows from any angle, then click Next."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_dna_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "aug_double_helix",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_dna_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "aug_antiparallel_arrows",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_dna_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "label_5prime_3prime",
              "poi": "strand_left_end",
              "text": "5' end"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_dna_model",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "label_5prime_3prime",
              "poi": "strand_right_end",
              "text": "3' end"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_helix_stage",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narration_antiparallel",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_dna_model",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            },
            {
              "target": "place_helix_stage",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_pairing"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_pairing",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "4. Build the Complementary Strand",
          "description": "A template strand (5'-ATGCCAGT-3') is shown with an empty complementary backbone beside it. Drag loose bases from the palette to complete the sequence using the base-pairing rules: A with T, G with C. Correct pairs snap into place and form hydrogen bonds \u2014 two dashed lines for A-T, three for G-C. Note that the new strand runs 3'-to-5', opposite the template. Complete the strand, then click Next."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_pairing_workstation",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "aug_pairing_activity",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_pairing_workstation",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "label_base_pairs",
              "poi": "palette_slot",
              "text": "A=T (2 H-bonds) \u00b7 G\u2261C (3 H-bonds)"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_pairing_workstation",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narration_pairing",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_pairing_workstation",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_helicase"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_helicase",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "5. Helicase Unwinds the Helix",
          "description": "Replication begins. Helicase, positioned at one end of the helix, moves along the DNA and breaks the hydrogen bonds between paired bases, opening the double helix at the replication fork and exposing the two template strands. Watch it work its way along the DNA. Point your device at the replication fork zone to continue as the next enzyme is added."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_replication_fork",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "aug_helicase",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_replication_fork",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "label_enzymes",
              "poi": "enzyme_label_slot",
              "text": "Helicase \u2014 unwinds & breaks H-bonds"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_replication_fork",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narration_fork",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_replication_fork",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "detect",
            "id": "thing_replication_fork"
          },
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_ssb"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_ssb",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "6. Single-Strand Binding Proteins Stabilize",
          "description": "As helicase separates the strands, single-strand binding proteins (SSBs) \u2014 shown as small labeled spheres \u2014 attach to each exposed strand. Single-stranded DNA is unstable and tends to re-pair or fold; the SSBs hold the strands open and accessible so replication can proceed. Click Next to add DNA polymerase."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_replication_fork",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "aug_helicase",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_replication_fork",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "label_enzymes",
              "poi": "enzyme_label_slot",
              "text": "SSBs \u2014 hold strands open & stable"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_replication_fork",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narration_fork",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_replication_fork",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_leading"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_leading",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "7. DNA Polymerase & the Leading Strand",
          "description": "DNA polymerase adds nucleotides only to the 3' end, so it reads a template 3'-to-5' while building the new strand 5'-to-3'. On the leading-strand template \u2014 oriented so synthesis follows the fork \u2014 polymerase moves continuously, adding free nucleotides that pair with the template. (A primer provides the starting point.) Watch the leading strand grow continuously, then click Next."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_replication_fork",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "aug_replication_fork",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_replication_fork",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "label_enzymes",
              "poi": "enzyme_label_slot",
              "text": "DNA polymerase \u2014 leading strand (continuous)"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_replication_fork",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narration_fork",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_replication_fork",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_lagging"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_lagging",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "8. The Lagging Strand & Okazaki Fragments",
          "description": "On the opposite template \u2014 whose 3' end points away from the fork \u2014 polymerase cannot follow the fork continuously. It works backward in short bursts, jumping back to the fork to start each new fragment. These short Okazaki fragments are then joined by DNA ligase into a continuous strand. Watch the fragments form and seal together, then click Next to view the whole fork."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_replication_fork",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "aug_replication_fork",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_replication_fork",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "label_enzymes",
              "poi": "enzyme_label_slot",
              "text": "DNA polymerase + ligase \u2014 lagging strand (Okazaki fragments)"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_replication_fork",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narration_fork",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_replication_fork",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_semiconservative"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_semiconservative",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "9. Semiconservative Replication \u2014 Predict the Rounds",
          "description": "The complete fork shows all five enzymes working together, and the asymmetry between strands traces directly back to the antiparallel structure. Now a parental molecule (gold strands) replicates once, producing two daughters each with one gold and one silver strand \u2014 this is why replication is 'semiconservative.' Predict the composition after a second round, then click Next for the recap."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "thing_replication_fork",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "aug_semiconservative",
              "poi": "default"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_replication_fork",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "label_enzymes",
              "poi": "enzyme_label_slot",
              "text": "Round 2 \u2192 2 hybrid + 2 fully-silver molecules"
            },
            {
              "target": "thing_replication_fork",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "narration_semiconservative",
              "poi": "default"
            }
          ],
          "deactivate": []
        },
        "exit": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [],
          "deactivate": [
            {
              "target": "thing_replication_fork",
              "type": "*",
              "augmentation": "*"
            }
          ]
        },
        "triggers": [
          {
            "mode": "click",
            "id": "step_recap"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "step_recap",
        "instruction": {
          "title": "10. Recap \u2014 Six Big Ideas",
          "description": "Review the six concept areas: (1) the double-helix structure with sugar-phosphate backbones and inward-facing bases; (2) complementary base-pairing rules with hydrogen-bond counts (A=T, G\u2261C); (3) antiparallel 5'-to-3' strand orientation; (4) the replication enzymes at the fork; (5) leading vs. lagging strand asymmetry with Okazaki fragments; and (6) semiconservative replication. Click Finish to end the lab."
        },
        "enter": {
          "remove_self": false,
          "activates": [
            {
              "target": "place_summary_board",
              "type": "predicate",
              "augmentation": "aug_summary_image",
              "poi": "default"
            },
